window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][52] = "<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Walk me through a product you took from an idea to a launch.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c30-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I led a project to build a new feature. We talked to users, wrote requirements, built it over a few months and launched. It went well and users liked it.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> no evidence, no assumption, no thresholds, no metrics, no learning.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;We saw that 40% of new users dropped at one step, so I interviewed ten users and found they didn't trust the data request. The riskiest assumption was that a clearer explanation would fix it, so we tested a prototype with 12 users and then an A/B test with 5% of traffic. I wrote a one-page spec with goals and non-goals, we launched in stages and completion went from 52% to 58%. I learned to test earlier.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c30-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I set the kill criteria before the test: proceed at a four-point gain, stop under one, with a guardrail on support contacts. The result was in the middle zone at week one and I extended the test rather than moving the threshold. The spec had two explicit non-goals, one of which (a redesign of the whole step) was requested by two stakeholders and declined with the data. At launch, completion rose six points and support contacts were flat. The retrospective found that my first interviews all came from active users; I now include lapsed users, and in the next project that changed the problem we solved. I can show the metric tree and the spec if useful.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c30-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence for the problem and a named riskiest assumption</td>\n<td>Kill criteria set in advance and a rule for the middle zone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A staged test and a metric</td>\n<td>Non-goals with the stakeholder pressure behind them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A lesson</td>\n<td>A pattern, a changed practice and its use in the next project</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c30-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report product-design and execution questions that ask for a user, a hypothesis and a launch plan with metrics <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: tell the journey with a clear user and evidence, a hypothesis, a test and a decision rule.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Meta's published values emphasize moving fast and focusing on long-term impact <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Meta's careers culture page, six core values]</code>. Candidates commonly report execution questions that probe speed with evidence <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: show the smallest test that could fail and the guardrail you used when you moved fast.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft sells to organizations and builds platforms, and candidates commonly report questions about customers, adoption and enterprise requirements <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: use journeys 2 and 4 as models: the buyer, the user, the administrator and the non-feature requirements.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes a principle on customer obsession and describes working backwards from the customer <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles; working-backwards material]</code>. Candidates commonly report detailed probing of a project's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: have the numbers for each stage and the customer evidence behind the decision.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Apple does not publish its development process, and candidates commonly report craft and judgment questions <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: tell what you left out (non-goals) and why, without disclosing confidential details of past work.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix describes a culture of candor and context and widely uses experiments <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo; Netflix Tech Blog]</code>. Candidates commonly report questions on testing and decision rules <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: lead with how the test and the decision rule worked, including when the result was ambiguous.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe is widely described as valuing clear writing and rigor <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager]</code>. Candidates report questions on spec quality and reliability <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: bring the spec excerpt, with the acceptance criteria and non-goals, as an artifact.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c30-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A list of activities, not decisions</td>\n<td>&quot;We did research, then design, then build&quot;</td>\n<td>Name each decision and its evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A spec written after the build</td>\n<td>Requirements match what was built exactly</td>\n<td>Write the spec from the evidence before building</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Metrics chosen after results</td>\n<td>Thresholds that fit the outcome</td>\n<td>Write the kill-criteria card first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>No middle zone</td>\n<td>Only pass or fail</td>\n<td>Decide what a mixed result triggers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>No non-goals</td>\n<td>Everything is in scope</td>\n<td>Name what you will not do and why</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The launch as the finish line</td>\n<td>No review after launch</td>\n<td>Success criteria and a review date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>A memorized journey</td>\n<td>Collapses at the second probe</td>\n<td>Use your own project; know the numbers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>A tidy story that hides the mess</td>\n<td>No false starts or reversals</td>\n<td>Say where you were wrong and what you changed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Ignoring non-feature requirements</td>\n<td>Security, consent, regulation appear late</td>\n<td>Put them in the plan at the end of discovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>A retrospective of virtues</td>\n<td>&quot;I learned to communicate&quot;</td>\n<td>A pattern, a practice and a check</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c30-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> is from C02.</p>\n<p><strong>Staged risk reduction</strong> (<code>MM-staged-risk</code>). Spend the least on the most uncertain assumption first and increase spend as risk falls: a manual test before a build, a pilot before a launch, draft-only before autonomy. Each phase gate (FW-C30-02) is a decision to spend more.</p>\n<p><strong>Multiplication of reliability</strong> (<code>MM-chain-reliability</code>). When steps depend on each other, probabilities multiply: five steps at 92% to 99% give about 79%. Check each step and the chain, and put the human check where the product of errors is largest.</p>\n<p><strong>Inversion</strong> (<code>MM-inversion</code>, see <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a>, C02). To plan a journey, ask how it would fail: wrong problem, wrong sample, a metric that can be gamed, a review that is a click. Design a check for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Pre-commitment</strong> (<code>MM-pre-commitment</code>). Writing the rule before seeing the result protects against moving the goalposts <code>[V: Nosek, Ebersole, DeHaven and Mellor, The Preregistration Revolution, PNAS 115(11), 2018, pp. 2600 to 2606: define the plan before observing outcomes]</code>. Kill-criteria cards are pre-commitment devices.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers want the spec before the build, with non-goals and acceptance criteria, and they will tell you which assumption is technically riskiest: ask them at the end of discovery. They dislike scope that grows without a tradeoff.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Designers and researchers lead discovery and the prototype tests; involve them in choosing the riskiest assumption and the sample. A stratified sample (journey 1) is their kind of correction.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Data scientists help set thresholds, sample sizes and intervals and review the metric tree; they dislike thresholds set after the data. Agree the kill-criteria card with them before the test.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal, security and operations.</strong> They own the non-feature requirements (consent, security review, regulation, reliability); engage them at the end of discovery, not at launch.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c30-checklist-before-you-tell-or-plan-a-product-journey\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: before you tell or plan a product journey</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the problem supported by evidence, with numbers?</li>\n<li>Have I named the riskiest assumption and why it was riskiest?</li>\n<li>Was the test the cheapest that could fail, with thresholds and a middle zone written first?</li>\n<li>Does the spec have a problem statement, goals, non-goals and acceptance criteria?</li>\n<li>Is there a metric tree with a primary measure and guardrails?</li>\n<li>Did I decide by the pre-set rule, and say how I used judgment in the middle zone?</li>\n<li>Did I include non-feature requirements early?</li>\n<li>Does my retrospective name a pattern, a practice and a check?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c30-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c30-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C30-01 · The memorized journey · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented (invented).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> A candidate preparing for a product interview learns Journey 2 from this chapter nearly word for word and, in a mock, tells it as if it were their own project. The interviewer asks about the time study, the third integration and how the security review was handled.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two weeks to prepare; one real project that the candidate considers dull; a mock interviewer who probes for detail.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> In the mock the candidate answers the first two probes by repeating the journey, fails the third (the candidate cannot say who did the time study or what changed), and the interviewer asks six follow-up questions: three are answered with specifics, two with generalities and one with a figure that contradicts the earlier one. The mock scorecard rates evidence as weak and credibility as low.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> As an observable problem: &quot;The candidate told an invented story they did not own, and could not answer the second layer of questions.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> The journeys are teaching devices (C30.1); the habits they show (a riskiest assumption, thresholds set first, non-goals, a retrospective) transfer, and the content does not. The candidate's real project had the same stages, with a smaller scale, and contained facts the candidate knew in detail: a funnel drop of 38% at one step, ten interviews and a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Keep telling the memorized journey</td>\n<td>Fluency</td>\n<td>Collapses under probing; risks dishonesty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Rebuild the answer from the candidate's own project with the journey template (FW-C30-01), with real numbers and one honest mess</td>\n<td>Credibility and depth</td>\n<td>Preparation time; a smaller-looking story</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Use a hypothetical, labeled as such</td>\n<td>Honesty with no real project</td>\n<td>Less proof of past impact</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs a few hours and survives probing; A is faster and fails the second question; C is acceptable when there is no real project and is labeled.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Choose B. Fill in the template with the real project, with the real numbers and one thing that went wrong and the practice changed; rehearse the probes (&quot;how did you know that?&quot;, &quot;what would have made you stop?&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, the rebuilt answer used a smaller project (a funnel drop of 38%, ten interviews, a 12-user prototype test, a 5% A/B test) and the follow-ups were answered with specifics in 7 of 8 cases; the scorecard rated evidence as strong (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the candidate's real project had been too thin, a clearly labeled hypothetical built on the template, plus a real smaller story about one stage, would have been honest and useful. The lesson: use the journeys to learn the habits, then tell your own story.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c30-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C30-02 · A public phase-gate model: discovery, alpha, beta and live in UK government digital services · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): the GOV.UK Service Standard page and Government Digital Service (GDS) blog posts of January 2014, June 2014 and September 2017 and the 2020 post on assessments after launch (primary); an archived page of the Government Service Design Manual in a government code repository (primary); a UK department's service-assessment page and its April 2026 blog post (primary); a New South Wales government toolkit page and a Scottish government page that restate the phases (primary, other governments). A consultant's history of the Service Standard gave 25 points for the first version, which the GDS posts contradict (26), and was not used. The GOV.UK service manual's own phase pages were not opened; the phases are read from the department and state pages. The analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> The UK Government Digital Service (GDS) publishes a Service Standard and a service manual to help teams build public digital services. The standard that GOV.UK lists today has 14 points <code>[V: GOV.UK Service Standard page]</code>. Its first version, the Digital by Default Service Standard, had 26 criteria and formally came into force in April 2014, when the government committed that all new or redesigned digital services would meet it before going live; GDS assessed the major services and departments assessed the smaller ones at each stage (alpha, beta and live) <code>[V: GDS blog posts, January and June 2014]</code>. GDS cut the 26 points to 18 in 2015 <code>[V: GDS blog, 2017]</code> and the standard was later reduced to the 14 points listed today. The delivery model has four phases: discovery, alpha, beta and live <code>[V: a department's service-assessment page; state and Scottish government pages]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Discovery</em> is about understanding users and their needs, constraints and the problem space; <em>alpha</em> is for testing different solutions to the problems found in discovery; <em>beta</em> is for building the best idea from alpha, starting with a private beta for limited users and moving to a public beta open to anyone; <em>live</em> is for supporting and continuing to improve the service <code>[V: department's service-assessment page]</code>. Formal assessments occur at the end of alpha, between private and public beta, and before live, with an informal peer review at the end of discovery <code>[V: department's service-assessment page; GDS blog, 2020]</code>. A GDS post of 2020 notes a gap: after the live assessment there was no planned formal engagement with service teams, although the manual expects continuous improvement in the live phase <code>[V: GDS blog, 2020]</code>. A department added a fifteenth point on sustainability for its own assessments in April 2026 <code>[V: department blog, 2026]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Many departments with different capabilities building many services; public money and a need for services to be accessible to everyone; a risk of large, late projects that deliver the wrong thing; and a need for an assurance process that fits agile work and does not become a bureaucracy.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As reported: the phases and what each is for; where assessments occur; the changes to the standard's number of points; the live-phase gap noted by GDS; and adoption of the same phases elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The model addresses an old problem in large projects: committing the whole budget before the riskiest assumptions are tested. As an observable problem: &quot;How to stage spending and scrutiny so that evidence, not a plan, earns the next phase?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis, through this chapter's tools.</em></p>\n<p><em>It is staged risk reduction (MM-staged-risk).</em> Discovery spends little to understand the problem; alpha tests solutions to the riskiest assumptions with prototypes; private beta tests with real users in a limited setting; public beta scales; each assessment is a gate where the team shows evidence from the previous phase before getting permission and funding for the next. This is the structure of every journey in this chapter, and of FW-C30-02.</p>\n<p><em>The gates demand evidence, not plans.</em> An archived page of the Government Service Design Manual says that to pass the beta assessment a team must explain how it has built on its work and evidence from the discovery, alpha and private beta phases <code>[V: archived government manual page]</code>. That is the equivalent of the kill-criteria card: criteria stated, evidence shown, decision made.</p>\n<p><em>Where it can fail <code>[Inf]</code>.</em> A gate can become a form to complete; the assessment can be passed without the team believing in the evidence; and the live phase, where most of a service's life is spent, has had the least formal review (the 2020 GDS post acknowledges this). The same weakness appears in product teams that celebrate a launch and stop measuring (C29.4).</p>\n<p><em>What the evidence does not show.</em> How many services were stopped or changed at a gate, the cost savings, or how consistent assessors were. The case supports an analysis of the phase-gate structure and its limits, not a claim that it guarantees good services.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a central digital team could face; not a record of its deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Approve whole projects up front on a business case</td>\n<td>Predictability of spend</td>\n<td>Commits before evidence; large late failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Fund in phases with assessments at gates and a published standard</td>\n<td>Evidence before spend; learning; consistency</td>\n<td>Process overhead; the risk of box-ticking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Leave it to each department</td>\n<td>Autonomy</td>\n<td>Inconsistent quality and accessibility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B costs process and delivers evidence-led funding; A is simpler and bets more on a plan; C is flexible and uneven.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the central digital team published a phased model and a standard, and the government committed that new or redesigned digital services would meet the standard before going live <code>[V: GDS blog posts, January and June 2014]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: the model remains in use, a department added a point to the standard in 2026 <code>[V: department blog]</code>, and other governments describe the same phases (New South Wales and Scotland) <code>[V: state and Scottish government pages]</code>. Effects on delivery outcomes are not described in the sources read.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> A model with the gates but no exit criteria would be a calendar; a model with criteria but no live-phase review would repeat the launch-as-finish-line anti-pattern. A reader should avoid hindsight and uncritical admiration: a public process earns trust by publishing its standard, and its value depends on how it is used. The method to carry away: stage spend by evidence, write exit criteria before each phase, and keep reviewing after launch.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Eight questions. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code> (composite candidates), and every figure is invented (invented). Each ends with follow-ups and answers. No company tag is attached because the topic is not tied to a single company.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-01 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you take a new product idea from discovery to launch?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Research, design, build, launch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Stages with evidence and a test before the build.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the riskiest assumption, a cheap test with thresholds set first, a spec with non-goals, a metric tree with guardrails, a staged rollout and a review after launch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd stage it so that spend follows evidence. Discovery: understand the user and the problem with interviews, observation and data, and list the assumptions; the riskiest is usually that people will pay or change behavior. Alpha: test that assumption with the cheapest experiment that can fail, such as a smoke test or a manual version, with the thresholds written first (proceed, stop, and what a middle result triggers). Spec: a short document with the problem statement, goals, non-goals and acceptance criteria. Metrics: a tree with a primary measure, the drivers and a guardrail. Beta: a staged rollout, private then wider, with a rollback trigger. Live: a review at a set date against the success criteria, and a retrospective that records the pattern and what I'd change.</p>\n<p>&quot;At each gate I'd decide to proceed, persist, pivot or stop by the rules written before the evidence arrived.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence-gated stages; thresholds first; non-goals; a review.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if you have no time for alpha? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd shrink it: a day of customer calls and a fake-door test can test the riskiest assumption cheaply. Skipping it entirely means committing before evidence; I'd say so and set a kill criterion for the first release.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you decide the thresholds? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;From the economics and the cost of being wrong: what conversion or retention makes the product worthwhile, and a stop line below which it isn't, with a middle zone for a better test. I agree them with the data scientist and the stakeholders before the test.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-02 · Beginner · execution · EX</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> What is the difference between discovery, alpha, beta and live?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Early, middle and late stages.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> The purpose of each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the evidence needed to exit each phase, the decision at each gate and that the live phase keeps reviewing; notes that public-sector digital teams use this model.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Discovery is for understanding users, constraints and the problem; its exit is evidence of the problem, the riskiest assumptions and a plan for testing them. Alpha is for testing different solutions to those assumptions, usually with prototypes; its exit is a tested solution with results against thresholds. Beta builds the best option and tests it with real users, first a limited private group and then everyone; its exit is a working end-to-end service with metrics, guardrails and operational readiness. Live runs and keeps improving the service, with reviews of outcomes and a plan for the next cycle. Public-sector digital teams, such as the UK government's, publish this four-phase model with assessments at gates, and it maps well onto product work.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Purpose and exit evidence for each; gates; continued review.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Can a phase be skipped? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A small, reversible change might combine them, but skipping alpha on a large bet commits spend before the riskiest assumption is tested. I'd compress, not skip, and record what I did not test.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What happens after live? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The same loop continues for smaller pieces of work: new discovery on the next problem, tests, releases. The common failure is stopping the review after launch, so I put a review date in the plan.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-03 · Intermediate · product-sense · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You are building a learning app for students in a market with low-end phones and weak data. What goes into the first version?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Videos and quizzes.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the job and constraints.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the riskiest assumptions (trust and willingness to pay), tests them manually first, designs for data and device limits, grounds AI answers in the textbook, and sets accuracy criteria by stratum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd start from the job: students stuck on a topic with no one to ask at night. The riskiest assumptions are that they and their parents will trust the answers and that parents will pay. I'd test that manually before building: a concierge test on a messaging channel for 40 students for four weeks, with drafts reviewed by a teacher, and thresholds written first (for example, at least 90% of reviewed answers correct, at least 3 questions per student per week, at least 10% of parents paying after two free weeks).</p>\n<p>&quot;First version: answers generated from the textbook passages with the page cited, a fallback to 'ask your teacher' when unsure, offline caching of the last answers, under 3 MB of data a session, and a 95th-percentile response under six seconds on a slow connection. Not in version one: videos, gamification, grade prediction. Accuracy criteria by difficulty stratum, not just an average, since an average can hide a weak chapter. Guardrails: wrong-answer reports, data cost, parent complaints, late-night use.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Job first; riskiest assumptions; manual test; device constraints; stratified accuracy.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Why not start with the videos students asked for? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;They asked for a solution; the job is getting unstuck. Videos are expensive and don't resolve a specific doubt at night. I'd test what resolves doubts fastest with the concierge version, and add video if the data shows it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if accuracy is 91% in the test? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That is not enough to scale. I'd ground answers in the textbook, add a fallback, and retest with a stratified sample until the weakest stratum meets the bar; and I'd tell users what the tool can't do.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-04 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you start a marketplace connecting small neighborhood shops with wholesalers?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build an app and onboard both sides.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts in one district and tests manually.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the supply behavior as the riskiest assumption, measures liquidity (acceptance within a time, fill rate, repeat), computes concierge economics, and notes regulatory limits on credit and payments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd start in one district and run it by hand. The riskiest assumption is supply behavior: will wholesalers accept orders through a platform and keep price lists current? I'd take 30 shops' orders on a messaging app in their language and phone wholesalers myself for six weeks, with thresholds written first: at least 60% of orders accepted within 30 minutes (stop under 50%), at least half the shops ordering three times (stop under 40%).</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd measure liquidity, not sign-ups: the share of orders accepted within 30 minutes, the fill rate and repeat ordering, with disputes and credit defaults as guardrails. Economics: with a 2% fee on a ₹4,500 order (₹90) against ₹95 of concierge cost and ₹18 of default cost, contribution is negative ₹23 an order, so the automation targets are the manual coordination steps: bringing cost under about ₹35 makes it about ₹37 positive. I'd keep credit to a ledger that only records what wholesalers extend until I've checked the rules on lending and payments, and stay in the district until acceptance holds above 75%.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> One district; liquidity measures; economics; regulatory caution.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if wholesalers won't update price lists daily? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That would be the finding that matters. I'd test lower-effort ways (photo of the list, a morning confirmation prompt), and if they won't, shops can't get reliable prices and the marketplace doesn't work as designed; I'd pivot to a different supply arrangement.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you expand? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;To a second district only when acceptance and repeat hold, using what the manual work showed about which steps to automate, and repeating the concierge start there with fewer coordinators.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-05 · Intermediate · strategy-gtm · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your enterprise pilot succeeded at three sites. Why might the deal still stall, and what do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The customer might not have budget.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the buyer, security and integration.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Maps the buying committee, plans the non-feature requirements early, plans for a seven-month sales cycle, prices to the buyer's logic, and sets the pilot's exit criteria to include procurement and security.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;A pilot proves the users like it; the deal needs the economic buyer, security, IT and procurement. It stalls when a security review or a single sign-on or audit-log requirement appears late, when the champion lacks budget authority, or when integration beyond the pilot system is needed. In one project a security review added about ten weeks because the documentation wasn't ready.</p>\n<p>&quot;So I'd map the buying committee at the start of the pilot (economic buyer, champion, administrators, IT and security, procurement and legal), put the security and compliance work into the plan at the end of discovery, write exit criteria that include procurement and security milestones, and give the buyer a business case in their terms: handling time down 27%, about 0.7 hours a shift saved per supervisor, against $1,800 per site per month (the figures here are from Journey 2 in C30, all invented). I'd plan for the real sales cycle (about seven months) and commit to outcomes and ranges to sales, not dated features.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Buying committee; non-feature requirements early; a business case; realistic cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if two of three pilot sites convert and one does not? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd learn why: the site's fit, the supervisor adoption or an integration limit. A third that doesn't convert may show a segment where the product doesn't fit, which is useful for targeting.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you price it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Per site, for predictable bills and a simple sale, with expansion by site count; I'd test per-exception pricing only if usage varies a lot between sites.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-06 · Hard · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> An agent drafts purchase orders and a person approves each one. The median approval takes three seconds. Is that safe?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, a person approves everything.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions whether reviewers really check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests reviewers with seeded errors, redesigns the approval screen to surface changes and uncertainty, tracks time and edits, and ties autonomy to supervised evidence by band with demotion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;Not necessarily: approval time of three seconds suggests many approvals are clicks, and a human in the loop only helps if the human is checking. I'd test it: seed a known set of errors into drafts, say 50, and see how many reviewers catch. In one test they caught 30 of 50 (60%); after changing the screen to highlight changed and low-confidence fields, they caught 41 of 50 (82%). I'd run that test monthly as a standing check.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd also track approval time, edits and override rates, not only acceptance. For autonomy, I'd use a ladder: draft-only for everything first; then, for a narrow band such as low-value reorders from an allow-listed vendor, autonomy after 300 supervised cases with zero errors (which bounds the error rate near 1%), and automatic demotion to draft-only on any error. Controls around it: untrusted-text handling against prompt injection, spending caps, an audit log and a kill switch.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Reviewer testing; screen design; time and edits; earned autonomy; controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Won't the extra review slow everything? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A better screen makes review faster and more real: reviewers look at the three fields that changed, not the whole document. I'd measure review time and catch rate together.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would make you promote a second band? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;The same evidence standard: a supervised run with zero errors at the required sample, a clear definition of the band, and a demotion rule. I'd promote slowly and tell finance and procurement what changed.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-07 · Hard · ai · AI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You run an internal AI evaluation platform used by 40 product teams. You cannot mandate adoption. How do you drive it, and how do you measure success?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Promote it and track how many teams use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Reduces friction and measures value.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats teams as customers, measures regressions caught rather than activity, keeps time to first run low, calibrates judges, uses narrow mandates only where risk is high, and checks that the metric can't be gamed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> &quot;I'd treat the teams as customers. Interview non-adopters to find the barrier; in one case it was setup time, so I'd cut time to first evaluation run to under a day with a template. I'd show value with results: in an 8-week pilot with 5 teams, 4 ran evaluations weekly and caught 11 regressions, 3 of which would have shipped. I'd calibrate any model-based judge against human labels before offering it (for example, 62.5% recall of bad outputs rose to 77.5% with claim-level checks).</p>\n<p>&quot;For measures, the headline is regressions caught that would have shipped and coverage of known failure modes, not evaluations run, since teams can inflate that with trivial runs. Adoption goes from 12 of 40 to 25 through templates, office hours and visible wins, with no mandate except for high-risk launches where the risk function requires evidence. Guardrails: reliability, cost per run, support load and team satisfaction. Each quarter I'd check that the headline can't be raised by trivial runs.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Customer view; value measures; calibrated judge; narrow mandate; anti-gaming check.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if a big team refuses? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd learn why and fix what's fixable, show them the regressions found elsewhere and offer a joint pilot. If they are in a high-risk category, the risk process can require evidence; otherwise their choice stands and I'd keep the door open.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you decide build versus buy for components? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Buy what is commodity (model access, storage), build what differs for us (workflow, calibration, permissions), and revisit when the market or our needs change.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c30-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C30-08 · Hard · behavioral · BE</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Tell me about a product you took from idea to launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A chronological description with no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Evidence, a test and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A through-line with a riskiest assumption, thresholds set first, a spec with non-goals, a decision by the pre-set rule, and a retrospective with a pattern and a changed practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>(A composite example; the figures are invented.)</em> &quot;We saw that 38% of new users dropped at a permissions step. I interviewed ten users and found they didn't understand why we needed access, so the riskiest assumption was that a clearer explanation would fix it. We tested a prototype with 12 users, then an A/B test on 5% of traffic with thresholds written first: proceed at a four-point completion gain, stop under one, and a guardrail on support contacts. The spec had two non-goals: no redesign of the whole flow, and no change to the permissions themselves.</p>\n<p>&quot;At week one the result was in the middle zone (a 2.5-point gain), so I extended the test two weeks rather than moving the threshold, and it reached 4.6 points. We launched in stages; completion rose from 52% to 58% and support contacts were flat. The retrospective found that my first interviews were all active users, which could have hidden why lapsed users never returned; I now include lapsed users in discovery, and in the next project that changed the problem we picked. I can show the metric tree and the spec.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Evidence; riskiest assumption; pre-set thresholds; non-goals; pattern and changed practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would you have done if the test had been under one point? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Stopped and told the stakeholders what we learned: that explanation wasn't the barrier. I'd have gone back to the interviews to find the real reason, and recorded the result.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Who disagreed, and how did you handle it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Two stakeholders wanted the whole step redesigned. I showed the funnel data (the drop was at one screen), agreed the test as the way to decide, and committed to revisit if the gain was small. They accepted because the rule was written first.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c30-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Eight exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Every figure is invented.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-01 · A kill-criteria card</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You will test a service that sends a weekly meal plan to busy parents by text message, run by hand for 30 families for four weeks. Write the kill-criteria card (FW-C30-03) with three measures, thresholds, sample, date and a middle-zone decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Alpha testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C30-03 and C26.6.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (1) <em>Families who still use the plan in week 4:</em> proceed at 50% or more (15 of 30), stop under 25% (7 of 30); middle zone: interview the lapsed families and revise the plan format for a second month. (2) <em>Families who pay $5 a week after two free weeks:</em> proceed at 20% or more (6 of 30), stop under 7% (2 of 30); middle zone: test a lower price. (3) <em>Delivery cost per family-week:</em> proceed if under $3, stop if over $8; middle zone: list automation targets. Sample: 30 families, with a note that 30 gives wide intervals, so a result near a threshold is treated as middle zone. Date: written before week 1 and dated; review at week 4. Decision owner: the PM with the data partner.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a guardrail: complaints per family under 0.2 a week.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Small samples give rough answers; set thresholds with wide middle zones.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-02 · Chain reliability</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An agent has four steps with accuracies 96%, 90%, 95% and 98%. Compute the chance that all four are right. If a human reviewer catches 80% of errors before action, what is the residual error rate? Where would you put the check, and what would you test about the reviewer?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Agents and approvals.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> MM-chain-reliability and Worked example 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> All four right: 0.96 × 0.90 × 0.95 × 0.98 = about 80.4%; chain error 19.6%. With a reviewer who catches 80%: residual error 19.6% × 0.2 = about 3.9% (96.1% correct). Put the check after the weakest step (90%) if the steps can be reviewed separately, or at the end where the product of errors is largest and an error becomes an action. Test the reviewer: seed known errors and measure the catch rate; the 80% is an assumption until measured, and a three-second review suggests it may be lower.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute the residual if the reviewer catches 60%: 19.6% × 0.4 = 7.8%.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> More checks reduce errors and add time and cost; place them where an error costs the most.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-03 · Concierge economics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A manual ordering service charges a 2.5% fee on a ₹3,000 average order. Concierge cost is ₹80 per order; credit defaults run 0.5% of order value. Compute contribution per order. What cost per order gives a contribution of ₹30?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Economics of a manual start.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Fee: 0.025 × 3,000 = ₹75. Defaults: 0.005 × 3,000 = ₹15. Contribution: 75 − 80 − 15 = −₹20 per order. For ₹30 of contribution: 75 − 15 − cost = 30, so cost = ₹30 per order. Automating the manual coordination to ₹30 is the target; check whether it is feasible, and whether the fee or default rate could change instead.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Raise the fee to 3.5% (₹105): contribution at ₹80 cost is 105 − 80 − 15 = ₹10; test whether shops and wholesalers accept it.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A manual start loses money by design; the question is whether the path to a positive margin is credible.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-04 · Unit economics of an AI learning feature</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Students resolve 4.0 doubts a week; a month is 4.3 weeks. Cost per answer is ₹0.50; fixed cost per active student is ₹9 a month. 12% of active students pay ₹149 a month. Compute monthly cost per active student, revenue per active student and contribution. What conversion breaks even, and what do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Consumer AI economics.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 1 and C20.7.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Answers a month: 4.0 × 4.3 = 17.2; variable cost: 17.2 × 0.50 = ₹8.60; total cost: 8.60 + 9 = ₹17.60. Revenue per active student: 0.12 × 149 = ₹17.88. Contribution: ₹0.28, about break-even. Break-even conversion: 17.60 ÷ 149 = 11.8%. At 15% conversion, contribution is 22.35 − 17.60 = ₹4.75. What to do: raise conversion (stronger free-to-paid trigger, a school channel), lower cost per answer (caching common questions), or raise price after testing; check the sensitivity before scaling acquisition.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Cache the 30% most common answers and compute the new variable cost (₹6.02 at a 30% cache hit rate).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Heavier use raises cost per active student; light use lowers value; price to the middle.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-05 · A spec excerpt</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a spec excerpt (FW-C30-01 part 3) for a feature that summarizes customer support tickets for agents: a problem statement, three goals, three non-goals, four requirements and acceptance criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Spec practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Ten minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> C16 and the spec excerpts in section 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Problem:</em> &quot;Agents spend about four minutes reading a long ticket history before replying; they need a short, accurate summary.&quot; <em>Goals:</em> reduce time to first reply by 20%; at least 95% of summaries free of invented facts on a 300-ticket evaluation; at least 70% of agents using summaries weekly. <em>Non-goals:</em> sending replies automatically; summarizing across customers; storing summaries beyond the ticket's retention period. <em>Requirements:</em> summaries cite the ticket messages they draw from; the agent can edit or dismiss; personal data follows the existing retention and access rules; latency under 3 seconds for the 95th percentile. <em>Acceptance:</em> the evaluation set (with a check on invented facts), the latency and a pilot with two agent teams meeting the goals for four weeks; a rollback if the invented-fact rate exceeds 2% in production.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a guardrail: first-reply quality score must not fall.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Non-goals disappoint some requesters and keep the project deliverable.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-06 · A metric tree for an enterprise tool</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Draw a metric tree for an internal tool that helps sales staff find answers in company documents: a primary measure, three drivers, two guardrails, and the first driver you would examine if usage were high and satisfaction low.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Metrics practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C05-01 and C27.10.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Primary:</em> questions answered correctly per salesperson per week (correct means the user accepted the answer and did not re-ask within a day). <em>Drivers:</em> active salespeople × questions per person × answer success rate; success rate depends on retrieval quality, document freshness and answer accuracy. <em>Guardrails:</em> wrong-answer reports per 1,000 and time to find an answer (latency). If usage is high and satisfaction low, examine answer success rate by question type first: usage shows people try it, so a failure in accuracy or freshness for common questions would explain low satisfaction; then check the share of &quot;no answer&quot; responses.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a trust measure: the share of answers where users open the cited source.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A correct-answer measure needs a way to judge correctness; sample reviewed answers weekly.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-07 · A retrospective with the anti-pattern scan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team launched a feature on time. After six weeks, the metric was flat. Using the failure-story structure (FW-C29-03) and the catalog (C29), write a retrospective naming a pattern, the root cause in your control, the missed signal and a practice with a check. Invent two numbers and mark them (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Closing a journey.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C29-03 and C29.4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> &quot;The pattern was <em>shipped counted as success</em>: we hit the date and declared it done. The root cause in my control: I never wrote a success criterion or a review date. The signal I missed: adoption among the target segment was 4% after two weeks against a 15% goal (invented), visible on the dashboard but not on anyone's review list. Practice changed: every launch has a metric, a target and a review date written before the build; a four-week review is on the calendar at kickoff. Evidence: the next two launches were reviewed on time, and one was stopped after the review because adoption stayed under 5%, saving about three engineer-weeks (invented).&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a gate: no launch review in the calendar, no launch approval.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Reviews take time; a stopped project saves more than the review costs.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c30-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C30-08 · Apply the journey template to your own idea</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Fill in the journey template (FW-C30-01) for a real product idea or a product you shipped, with real numbers, thresholds, a spec excerpt, a metric tree and a retrospective. Then give it aloud in 30 minutes to a partner who probes.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Practice for the idea-to-launch question.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Your work.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two hours for the template; 30 minutes aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C30-01 and section 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> A good template has: a context with a named user and alternative; discovery with at least two sources of evidence and a named riskiest assumption; a test with a kill-criteria card written before the result and a middle zone; a spec excerpt with a problem statement, goals, non-goals and acceptance criteria; a metric tree with a primary measure and a guardrail; results and the decision by the pre-set rule; tradeoffs with alternatives; and a retrospective with a pattern, a practice and a check. In the spoken version, the partner should be able to ask &quot;how did you know that?&quot; at each stage and get a number or a source. Mark anything you cannot support as an assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If you have no shipped project, use a labeled hypothetical for the full template and a real, smaller story for one stage.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The template takes preparation and survives probing.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for two weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: stages and thresholds.</em> Use IQ-C30-01, IQ-C30-02, IQ-C30-03, EX-C30-01, EX-C30-02 and EX-C30-05. Fill in the journey template for a product idea, with a kill-criteria card written before you decide anything.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: economics, AI and the behavioral version.</em> Use IQ-C30-04 to IQ-C30-08 and EX-C30-03, EX-C30-04, EX-C30-06, EX-C30-07 and EX-C30-08. Compute the economics by hand, and rehearse the 30-minute idea-to-launch answer with a partner who probes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;How did you know that?&quot; and &quot;What would have made you stop?&quot; at random, and to ask about one non-goal and one thing that went wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I give evidence for the problem, with a number?</li>\n<li>Did I name the riskiest assumption and why?</li>\n<li>Were thresholds and a middle zone written before the result?</li>\n<li>Did the spec have non-goals and acceptance criteria?</li>\n<li>Did I give a metric tree with a guardrail?</li>\n<li>Did I decide by the pre-set rule and say where I used judgment?</li>\n<li>Did I include non-feature requirements early?</li>\n<li>Did my retrospective name a pattern, a practice and a check?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> Record the date, the prompt, the stage where I was weakest, the number I could not produce, my score and the one fix.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Use the journey template on a real project over three weeks. If you have no current project, use a feature bet or a side project.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Pick one product idea or one recently shipped feature. Fill in parts 1 and 2 of the template: context and users, and the discovery evidence with a named riskiest assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Write the kill-criteria card for the next test and the spec excerpt (problem statement, goals, non-goals, acceptance). Share both with an engineer and a data partner and ask what is missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run the test or review the result. Build the metric tree with a guardrail. Decide by the pre-set rule, and record the decision and the middle-zone judgment.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Write the tradeoffs and the retrospective with a pattern, a practice and a check. Record it as a one-page journey and rehearse it aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the completed template (a ready answer to &quot;tell me about a product you shipped&quot;), the kill-criteria card with its result (a time you decided by a rule written first) and the retrospective (a changed practice with a check). Keep every number real and credit partners.</p>\n<span id=\"c30-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c30-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>An end-to-end story has a through-line: evidence for the problem, the riskiest assumption, a cheap test with thresholds set first, a spec with non-goals and acceptance criteria, a metric tree with guardrails, a decision by the pre-set rule, and a retrospective that changes a habit.</li>\n<li>In all five journeys the riskiest assumption was not the build (willingness to pay and trust, the buying process, supply behavior, adoption, reliability and review), and a manual or narrow first version tested it cheaper than building.</li>\n<li>Non-feature requirements (security review, consent, payment and credit rules, permissions, injection defenses) shape the plan and belong in it at the end of discovery.</li>\n<li>Reliability multiplies down chains, reviewers must be tested with seeded errors, and autonomy is earned per band by supervised evidence with demotion on error.</li>\n<li>Public phase models (discovery, alpha, beta, live, with assessments) formalize staged risk reduction; use the gates, write the exit criteria first and keep reviewing after launch. Use the journeys to learn the habits and tell your own story.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c30-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The GOV.UK Service Standard page; GDS blog posts of 2014, 2017 and 2020 on the standard and on assessments; a UK department's service-assessment page; and the New South Wales and Scottish government pages that restate the phases. Sources for CS-C30-02 (confirmed in October 2026).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Eric Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em> (2011), and Marty Cagan, <em>Inspired</em> (2017), on testing assumptions before building <code>[V: Ries: Crown Business, 13 September 2011, ISBN 9780307887894; Cagan: 2nd edition, Wiley, December 2017]</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Chapters C05, C06, C16, C17, C24, C25, C26, C27 and C29 of this book, which teach the parts the journeys connect.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"part-ix\"></span>\n";
